    "title": "How many perfect brackets remain? March Madness first weekend leaves none standing",
    "text": "March 22, 2026Updated March 23, 2026, 10:09 p.m. ETWell, maybe next year.Even with what was widely derided as a relatively chalky opening weekend of March Madness, no perfect Men's NCAA Tournament brackets remain after the first weekend. The final torch was snuffed out when No. 6 Tennessee held off No. 3 Virginia, shattering the illusion that maybe this is the year.Fans already faced impossibly long odds of creating a perfect bracket. So having upsets on Day 1 took out a massive chunk of brackets. However, the aforementioned chalkiness of the ensuing games — with just one double-digit seed headed to the Sweet 16 — likely also eliminated a bunch of brackets.Blame Texas. Blame Gonzaga.So no perfect brackets remain in this year's March Madness. But hey, next year is definitely the year.How many people still have perfect bracket in March Madness?Last updated 10 p.m. ET on March 23.ESPN: 0 perfect brackets remain (beginning with 26,029,409)NCAA: 0 perfect brackets remainThe dream is dead. There will be no perfection in 2026 March Madness.Tennessee's defeat of Virginia was the death knell for the final perfect remaining. Of course, even if that one had endured, Florida's eventual loss to Iowa would derail nearly everyone else who was seriously picking. Such is the madness of March.",
